<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upper Mesopotamia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> is the northern part of the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, covering parts of present-day northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Rain-fed agriculture and trade routes supported cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, making it the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>. It was later contested by the Old Babylonian kingdom and, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>, ruled by branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia.jpg" alt="Upper Mesopotamia" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Schmaltz, MODIS Rapid Response Team, NASA/GSFC, Jazira highlights added by Rafy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jazira.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
